WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.790
RAFAEL SANCHEZ: My
name is Rafael Sanchez.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.590
I am Customer Engineer
of Google Cloud.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:11.990
I am based in Madrid.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.700
And I'm on here on the stage
with Jose Antonio Marcos, who

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.940
is the Head of the Smart
Maintenance Engineer in Talgo.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.790
We're going to talk to
you today about what

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.105
we think this is a very
interesting IoT use case.

00:00:23.105 --> 00:00:25.230
Because we are going to be
talking about high speed

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.250
trains operating
at 300 kilometers

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.970
per hour, around
200 miles per hour,

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.229
in the desert of Saudi Arabia.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.160
So you can imagine that they
have very strict weather

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.630
conditions there.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.560
And these trains operating
at 300 kilometers per hour

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.010
are going to send--

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.250
or are sending, actually, up
to 2,000 signals per second

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.850
to the cloud for processing
for predictive maintenance.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.920
So this is what we are
going to talk today.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.680
So, this case study is actually
a study around asset tracking

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.570
and asset management.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.210
And before describing
the case itself,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.550
I would like to start by
making some definitions, OK?

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.740
Knowing that no
definition is perfect,

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.300
I would like to
start by defining

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.610
what is an asset, what
is asset tracking,

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.130
and what is asset
management, OK,

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.660
in order to understand
the scope of what we

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.580
are going to talk to you today.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.160
So, let me start off with
a definition of an asset.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.450
An asset is a useful or valuable
thing or person, generally

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.110
owned by a person or
enterprise, available to perform

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.000
some utility.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.420
Asset tracking is basically
around two concepts

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.290
of these centers.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.950
Asset tracking is around
real-time awareness

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.370
of a valuable thing
that we own, OK?

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:40.745
So we'll have
some-- for example,

00:01:40.745 --> 00:01:43.490
in the train we'll have some
subsystems that we would like

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.500
to measure, something
valuable for us,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.060
something valuable for Talgo.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.430
And we would like to make
some real-time awareness

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.390
of that particular information.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.740
So, asset tracking is around
understanding these business

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.410
questions that you
see on this slide.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:57.560
So, where is my assets?

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.060
So, where is the
train, for example,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.500
or where are certain
parts of the train?

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.190
How does it get to go
to the destination?

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.510
Or what is the
current condition, OK?

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.370
An asset, in the
context of Talgo,

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.020
is not the train as a whole.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:14.020
It's a series of subsystems
that exist in the train

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.250
and that we would like
to make tracking of, OK?

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.130
So, and what is
asset management?

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.960
Asset management is
around performance,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.650
is around understanding
and managing

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.030
how an asset is being used.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.960
So we are trying to answer the
business question here, like,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.090
if some parts of the
train are likely to fail,

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.620
how efficiently it's being used,
or how a particular asset is

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.690
performing.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.450
So the question is,
how do we answer

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.330
those particular
business questions?

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.020
So, how do we get
the information

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.190
that we need from these
business questions?

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.830
So, what we think
is that we will

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.380
be able to answer these
business questions if we

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.640
collect the right amount of
data related to time and space.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.730
So if we are able to
have tools in order

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.470
to collect the right information
related with time and space,

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:07.800
we should be able to answer the
business question before, OK?

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.760
So regarding the-- so,
actually, the problem

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.660
of answering those
business question--

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.890
we have turned that particular
problem into a situation where

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.320
we need to collect the
right amount of information,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.140
the right amount of data,
related to time and space.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.110
So in our timing, we
are interested basically

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.400
in there periods of time.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.330
We get interested in the now,
in the current situation, OK?

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:34.080
And this is basically the
real-time collection of data--

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.290
data which can be associated
with temperature, either

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:40.950
the outside temperature or the
temperature of the differential

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.300
system in the train,
or can be, for example,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.070
voltage, or it
can be hydraulics,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.230
or can be some particular
things in the train

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.890
that we would like to measure.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:54.110
Past information is
just the information

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.090
we have already collected, OK?

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.580
So for example, in the
train-- this is, by the way,

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.250
the train which is in
production in Talgo.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:03.050
This is the line between
Medina and Mecca.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.520
So this is actually
the case study

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.000
we are talking about here.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.080
So in a train within
that route, we

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.470
are collecting
information in the past.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.600
And we are storing
that information, OK?

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.750
So this is the past period
that we are also interested.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.410
And so, basically,
the past periods

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.395
is just the information
and real-time information

00:04:23.395 --> 00:04:25.020
that we have collected
in the past, OK?

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.310
So, we can do these basically
with the same tools.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.300
And the future is
not information

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.880
that we are
collecting, of course.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.120
Those data does not exist.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.570
The future is basically
information that we predict.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:39.480
And we predict using machine
learning technologies.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.550
And we predict using
machine learning similarly

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.540
with the data that we
have already collected.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.510
We have data we have
collected in the past

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.930
and with the real-time
data we are collecting.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:52.470
So these are the
three periods of time,

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:56.140
and we need to
collect information.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.640
And these are on timing.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.940
And what about the space?

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.200
In the space, there has been
a change over the last years

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.130
related to space.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:08.910
Basically, there has to be
a shift from location data

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.830
into location intelligence.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.540
In the past, location was just
to put a spot on a map, OK?

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.980
So to put some
train, in this case,

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.590
in a particular map-- when
we moved to digital maps,

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:20.850
it was basically that.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.940
But in the last
years, there has been

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.000
a shift from location data
to location intelligence

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.540
where we add context
information around the location.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.930
So the location is not just
the latitude and the longitude

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.420
where the train is.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.690
It's an amount of information
around that particular location

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:42.800
where we can get some
intelligence from.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.460
So, in order to do this, in
order to collect information

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.670
from time, in order to collect
information from space,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.680
we need to have the right tools.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.900
We need to have the
right tools in the cloud.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:58.120
And Google Cloud has
very interesting tools

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.340
in order to collect information
relating to time and space.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:04.890
For space, we have our Google
Maps platform for enterprises,

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:05.480
OK?

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:10.520
And for timing, we have a
series of processing data tools

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.450
that allow us also to
collect information regarding

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:17.710
the time-- real-time awareness,
past information, et cetera.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.010
We can find these tools
as separate products--

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.350
tools for ingestion,
tools for processing,

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.060
tools for visualization.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.700
But also in Google Cloud-- just
to give you an example of some

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.150
of the problems that we have--

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:32.860
we have also problems that
combine time and space

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.400
information into just one.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.610
And I would like to show
you just one of it, which

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.718
is the BigQuery GIS.

00:06:38.718 --> 00:06:40.260
I should point out
you know BigQuery.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.600
BigQuery's our data
warehouse product.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:47.910
It's a serverless platform to
make queries over large data

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.960
sets at very competitive costs.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:52.150
It's a serverless platform.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:58.150
And we have introduced a new
feature, which basically allow

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.450
us to visualize
latitude and longitude

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:03.700
information within BigQuery.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.850
So we can build SQL queries
with geographical information

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:08.070
inside.

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:11.110
And we can visualize that
information dynamically

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:14.080
in the broadest sense, OK?

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.650
So, this is a sample of
a product that combines

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.270
timing and space information.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:24.250
And Talgo is actually using some
of the Google Cloud products

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:28.768
that allow to collect the timing
and space information, OK?

00:07:28.768 --> 00:07:30.310
And this is the use
case we are going

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:33.430
to talk to you about today.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.580
This case study was
published one month ago.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.520
You can access to the
case study in that URL--

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.190
cloud.google.com
/customers/talgo/,

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.410
where we described the
case study in detail,

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.500
and actually with some
quotes from Jose Antonio.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:54.100
And, well, we described
all the technology

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.650
that we were using in that.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.390
But I cannot think of a better
person to tell us more about

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:03.390
Talgo and to tell us more about
why Talgo have chosen Google

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.370
Cloud than Jose Antonio Marcos,
who will tell us more about

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:09.112
the case study.

00:08:09.112 --> 00:08:11.070
JOSE ANTONIO MARCOS
ALBERCA: Thank you, Rafael.

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.180
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.410
First of all, let me
introduce you to Talgo.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:23.550
Talgo is a high speed
manufacturing and maintenance

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:27.770
company funded in 1942.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:30.620
In their early days,
Talgo designed trains

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:32.809
with very innovative
features, which

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:35.730
is the backbone of the company.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:39.730
Actually, Talgo has
started to build trains--

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:46.990
light, aluminum
trains-- in USA in 1946,

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:50.170
using the aircraft
technology, aluminum,

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:52.840
because that
technology-- it wasn't

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.170
that available in Europe.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:01.630
77 years later, Talgo is
leader with this technology

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:05.440
in different countries.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:10.480
Nowadays, Talgo is
developing high-speed trains

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:18.300
running about 220 miles per
hour, 350 kilometers per hour.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:25.260
For example, this is their
new high-speed train in Spain.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.580
This new project
for AVRIL for High

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:32.550
Speed 2 in UK, AVRIL-UK,
and the high-speed train

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:35.250
for Saudi Arabia, which
is the case that I'm going

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:41.200
to present to you afterwards.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.600
Regarding Talgo technology,
the main features

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:47.820
comparing Talgo trends
with the standard ones

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.310
are, for example, independent
wheels, natural tilting,

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:57.070
aluminum light cars, lower floor
level, automatic gauge change,

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:00.810
and articulated cars.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:07.490
As general information, Talgo
main figures are as follows.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:12.660
Talgo has footprint in 44
countries, more than 4,000

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:17.970
vehicles, 77 years
of experience,

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:22.390
and we are performing more
than 1,000 million kilometers

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.500
per year.

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:28.990
So that means four times the
distance between the Earth

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:34.560
to the moon every year,
just to keep in mind.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.240
Now we get into the gates.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:43.850
Nowadays, high-speed trains'
maintenance operation

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:48.740
is very complex and high tech.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:54.150
We need to solve different
technology challenges.

00:10:54.150 --> 00:11:00.440
For example, safety first, no
questions; very high speed,

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:06.680
more than 220 miles per hour
or 350 kilometers per hour;

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:10.270
reliability, about
1 million miles

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:15.830
between a stop between delays;
availability, 100% the trains

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:20.600
running on the track;
and maintenance intervals

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:24.750
between the stops
every 30,000 miles.

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.680
So how to do it?

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:30.410
In order to reach
these challenges,

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:34.230
we had to work with new
maintenance technologies--

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:36.810
for example, CBM,
Condition-Based

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:39.360
Maintenance in
order to anticipate

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.720
the failures of
the train that may

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:48.380
result in lack of availability
and reliability of the train.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:50.690
To apply condition-based
maintenance,

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:55.250
we need to develop algorithms.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:58.430
These algorithms
need a lot of data

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:01.710
in order to work with
machine learning.

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.370
So in order to get
this data, we have

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:08.180
developed a real-time
monitoring system

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:11.925
using Google Cloud
platform that you

00:12:11.925 --> 00:12:13.640
are going to see afterwards.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:17.340
Rafael is going to show you.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:20.430
So now we get into
this successful case,

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:25.020
monitoring system for Saudi
Arabia high-speed trains.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.240
This project had
several challenges.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:32.850
For example, extreme
weather condition,

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:36.690
high temperature during
summertime more than 120

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:42.070
degrees Fahrenheit, extreme
weather conditions, sandstorms,

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:43.980
the geography.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:49.620
Another challenge
was to understand

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.510
how to send bigger stream data
from the train to the ground

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:55.720
control.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:58.980
And we had to decide how to
do it, not for this train,

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:02.110
but for the future trains.

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:06.290
So what were the challenges
that we had to deal with?

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:10.690
We had to deal with data
transmission ingestion

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:15.020
capacity, processing capacity,
and storage capacity--

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:17.540
all of these challenge.

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:19.070
How did we solve it?

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:21.560
We had to sell in from
different options.

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:25.400
On-premise, we need a huge
investment, other cloud

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:30.190
solution, and finally, we
decide to go with Google Cloud.

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:31.940
Why?

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:36.350
Because Google Cloud offered
us different advantages,

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:41.910
for example, unlimited
scalability, connectivity,

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:45.560
massive storage, streaming
data, and machine learning

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:48.780
for predictive maintenance.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:51.500
How is the process?

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:55.850
The train is sending data
in three different ways,

00:13:55.850 --> 00:14:00.760
using a UDP datagrams.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.530
It is streaming data,
live streaming data.

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:09.120
We are sending more than 2,000
signals per second per train.

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:12.330
Also, we are able to
download the whole database

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.240
when the train go to the
maintenance facilities

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:17.330
through Wi-Fi.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:21.271
I'm talking about 2
gigabytes per train per day.

00:14:21.271 --> 00:14:24.230
And also, we are sending
the batch alarms.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:27.070
Every 10 minutes, we are
sending the main alarms

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:29.330
and the main events
from the train.

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:32.670
So once we have the
data in the cloud,

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:35.780
ingest it, and store
it in BigQuery,

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:40.970
we are able to track the
trains you see in Google Maps.

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:45.610
Also, performing in
real-time monitoring.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:49.220
And we are doing
machine learning

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:53.960
on predictive maintenance
using our application, Talgo

00:14:53.960 --> 00:15:00.290
Tesla The acronym of Tesla is
Train Efficient System Logic

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:02.180
Analysis.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.402
So, now we are
going to see a video

00:15:04.402 --> 00:15:05.860
with the example
of the application

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:10.040
that we developed
using Google Maps.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:14.720
So here, we can see the trains--

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:17.100
the location of the trains.

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:23.760
We can find out the problem that
they are sending in real time.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.370
We can select different
data, different parameters,

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:32.210
to understand which is the
problem that the train is

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:35.790
having in order to get
the insight of the data.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:39.830
So we can choose from different
trains, different countries.

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:43.380
Now we are going
to see an example

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:45.500
with a video of the
application that we

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.660
developed using Google Cloud.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:49.520
This is real-time monitoring.

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:53.300
We are monitoring
different parameters

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.040
of the train-- the speed.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.700
And also, we are able
to monitor in real time

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:05.650
every single signals in order
to analyze what is going on

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:10.780
and to improve the availability
and reliability of the train.

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:17.930
OK, so now, Rafael
is going to show you

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:21.170
which is the architecture
of this case.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:23.050
RAFAEL SANCHEZ: OK,
thank you, Jose Antonio.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:24.925
So I'm going to show
you now the architecture

00:16:24.925 --> 00:16:26.370
which is in production today--

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:28.512
in production today in
the Saudi Arabia line, OK?

00:16:28.512 --> 00:16:29.970
So basically, the
architecture that

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:32.380
generate all these
videos and graphs

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:34.750
that we have seen before.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:37.090
So, I'm going to describe
the three architectures that

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:39.340
are in production today.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:41.290
One is about
real-time monitoring.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.710
The other one is about
the historical analysis.

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:46.092
And the third one is about
event data management.

00:16:46.092 --> 00:16:47.550
So let me start
with the first one.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.860
This is the one about
real-time monitoring

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:53.100
where the trains operating
at 300 kilometers per hour

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:59.100
are sending 2,000 UDP datagrams
per second to the cloud.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:03.420
So they are sending this by
using GSM-R technology, OK?

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:07.800
So GSM is-- I mean, it's a
public telephony standard.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.560
And there is a variant for
the railways obligation,

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.650
which in the GSM-R, actually
the R is for "Railway."

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:19.589
And this is a very well-known
railway standard, OK?

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:21.901
So the GSM-R has
the capabilities,

00:17:21.901 --> 00:17:23.859
or has very interesting
capability for somebody

00:17:23.859 --> 00:17:26.849
to operate at a
different frequency

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:28.920
band than the public GMS.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:30.630
So therefore, there
are no interference

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.990
with the public GSM technology.

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:37.420
It can also operate with trains
up to 500 kilometers per hour.

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:39.900
So it's good enough for
this particular obligation.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:43.140
And also, normally, we do
design a high speed line

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:47.790
with GSM-R. You have antennas
all over the lines, which

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:50.440
guarantees you the coverage
even inside the tunnels.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:50.940
OK?

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:54.300
So that's the reason why the
GSM-R is good enough technology

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:57.370
today for this
particular obligation.

00:17:57.370 --> 00:18:01.960
So, we are using GSM-R.
And then over a VPN, OK?

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:06.690
Sorry, over a VPN, we
are sending the signals

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:09.640
to a managed instance group
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:12.130
Actually, we are using an
internal load balancer--

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:13.870
an internal load
balancer here, which

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:18.160
basically balances the traffic
to the different computing

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:19.240
machines.

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:22.090
So after that,
all the traffic is

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:26.470
being sent to our queuing
system to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:29.990
And then it is going to
AppEngine for processing, OK?

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:34.060
So, we are not using Dataflow
on that particular application.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:37.700
We are using a Node.js
application built on AppEngine

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:41.300
which is basically
decoding the UDP packets.

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:46.317
And then the packets are going
for visualization in real time

00:18:46.317 --> 00:18:47.900
to another AppEngine
application which

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:50.405
is the one that makes
the visualization.

00:18:50.405 --> 00:18:52.650
And one interesting
capability is

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:55.670
that we are also
using a database here

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:58.070
in order to store all the
real-time information that we

00:18:58.070 --> 00:18:59.360
are receiving, OK?

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.170
So while there is here
a real-time path using

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:06.570
a WebSocket that visualizes
directly all the real-time

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:11.150
information into our graph,
we are also storing--

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:14.090
Talgo is also storing--
the historical information

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:16.830
coming from this stream.

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:21.890
So to summarize this
particular architecture,

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:23.390
so we are collecting
the information

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:25.820
using GSM-R with UDP datagrams.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.790
Then this goes to an
outer scalar group

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:31.880
built with an internal load
balancer on a managed instance

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:32.720
group.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:36.620
Then it goes to Pub/Sub for
collecting all the information,

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:38.900
AppEngine to decode
the UDP datagrams

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:41.150
and extract the useful
information, and finally

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:45.870
another AppEngine application
for visualization.

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:49.050
You may notice that, on this
particular architecture,

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.840
there is no Cloud
IoT Core, which

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:56.430
is one of the problems that
normally appears in an IoT

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:57.580
application like this.

00:19:57.580 --> 00:19:59.160
And the reason is the following.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:01.290
When we designed these
particular architectures,

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:04.950
the train were
already built. So you

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:07.560
can imagine that a train
like this takes a lot of time

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:09.690
to redesign and
to remanufacture.

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:11.430
And there is a lot
of security inside.

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:14.790
I mean, you cannot just come
here and put a software there

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:17.730
or a hardware there
because, I mean,

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:21.660
Talgo is making a very strict
moderation process for hardware

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:22.600
and software.

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:26.040
So basically, we didn't
arrive with enough time

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:28.420
in order to install
our Cloud SDK,

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:31.830
uninstall all the MQTT
client on the train

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:34.060
in order to be able to
talk to Cloud IoT Core.

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:37.500
Because Cloud IoT Core, as you
know, requires MQTT or HTTP

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:39.580
and requires also TLS 1.2.

00:20:39.580 --> 00:20:43.140
So we could not install all
the right software libraries

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.920
in order to deploy
that on the trains.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:49.750
The future architecture, I
will describe you afterwards.

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:52.895
They are-- we're including
Cloud IoT Core, of course, OK?

00:20:52.895 --> 00:20:54.270
So that's the
reason behind that.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:57.030
Basically, we didn't
have time in order

00:20:57.030 --> 00:20:59.540
to install and moderate
all the software

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:02.550
and to pass all of the
moderation tests required

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:03.990
by Talgo.

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:05.610
Because again, as
you can see, this

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:08.430
is a very strict device, OK?

00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:11.960
So we need to pass very, very
strict moderation process.

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:16.170
And we cannot just go
and do whatever we want.

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:19.190
So, this is the architecture
about real-time monitoring.

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:22.140
The next architecture is
about historical analysis.

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:27.560
In this case, the information is
sent when the train is stopped.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:29.610
It's stopped for maintenance.

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:32.440
It's stopped for maintenance
at a Talgo facility, OK?

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:33.440
So the train is stopped.

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:37.880
This happens normally once every
15 days or once every 20 days.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:44.780
And when that happens, the train
is sending 2 gigabyte files

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:48.470
per day of operation to a local
computer which is installed

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:50.340
on the Talgo facility.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:53.565
So again, you may ask, why
your local computers here?

00:21:53.565 --> 00:21:54.940
The reason is the
same as before.

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:57.530
We didn't have time to
add the necessary software

00:21:57.530 --> 00:22:00.380
on the train, our
Cloud SDK, et cetera,

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:02.600
in order to send
the data to GCS.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:07.340
But in the future
architecture, this is included.

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:09.160
So these files are--

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:13.070
again, these are
SQLite databases,

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:16.050
which are 2 gigabytes
size per day, which

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:17.290
is sent to a local computer.

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:19.930
In this local computer,
we have gsutil installed.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:22.510
Then the local computer
sends information

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:24.195
to cloud storage in gsutil.

00:22:24.195 --> 00:22:25.570
And then when that
happens, there

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:28.310
is a Cloud Function
which is triggered.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:32.860
And this trigger will launch
a Dataflow pipeline to process

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:35.210
all the SQLite database.

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:37.150
So the SQLite database
go to BigQuery

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:40.600
for storage and visualization
and to an AppEngine

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:44.960
for visualization of
the historical data.

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:49.300
So to summarize, we have Wi-Fi
data when the train is stopped,

00:22:49.300 --> 00:22:53.030
which is sent to a local
computer and cloud storage.

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:56.380
Then we are sending it
to a Cloud Function.

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:58.630
And a Cloud Function is
triggered for extraction, then

00:22:58.630 --> 00:23:03.563
Dataflow, and then
AppEngine for visualization.

00:23:03.563 --> 00:23:05.980
And the third architecture
which is in production in Talgo

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:11.520
is the one about event data
and alarm data collection.

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:14.330
So, this is not in real time
because this is actually

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:18.350
a batch type of
architecture because we are

00:23:18.350 --> 00:23:20.620
sending alarms every minute.

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:24.810
And we are sending events
every 10 minutes to the cloud.

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:28.140
So what is an event in
this particular context?

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:32.010
So, an event is triggered where
certain conditions are met.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:34.280
For example, imagine
that a temperature

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:38.570
is higher than 40 degrees, plus
that the voltage is exceeding

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:41.100
whatever value, plus
the train speed is

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:43.120
at 250 kilometers per hour.

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:45.980
If the three conditions
are met, then an event

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:48.105
is triggered and is
sent to the cloud.

00:23:48.105 --> 00:23:50.480
But we are not collecting
events and alarms in real time.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.800
We are collecting events
and alarms in batch mode.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:56.670
So alarms every minute, and
events every 10 minutes.

00:23:56.670 --> 00:24:00.640
So, the way we send this
information is again via GSM-R

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:03.847
using a VPN over TCP to
compute it in machine.

00:24:03.847 --> 00:24:05.430
We don't need
autoscaling in this case

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:08.626
because the amount of
traffic is relatively low.

00:24:08.626 --> 00:24:11.970
And then this is sent to
cloud storage for storing.

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:14.538
And then this
information is sent--

00:24:14.538 --> 00:24:17.080
I mean, there is an application
which is basically collecting

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:18.890
the information
from cloud storage,

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:22.370
and then sent to Pub/Sub,
Cloud Dataflow for processing,

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:26.202
BigQuery, AppEngine, and
finally visualizing here.

00:24:26.202 --> 00:24:29.020
The visualization is happening
in a tool called Cassandra,

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:32.200
which, by the way, has nothing
to do with a Cassandra NoSQL

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:32.820
database, OK?

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:35.130
So it's a customized
tool for Talgo.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:39.830
I mean, the name has
nothing to do with that.

00:24:39.830 --> 00:24:43.780
So to summarize, so
the events and alarms

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:47.650
are sent through
GSM-R in batch mode.

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:51.040
This is sent in cloud storage,
and then from cloud storage,

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:53.620
we start all the processing
and visualization

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:55.960
using the standard
pipeline processing

00:24:55.960 --> 00:25:02.050
with Dataflow, Pub/Sub, and
BigQuery until visualization.

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:06.730
OK, so until now, I
have described the three

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:09.790
architectures which
are in production today

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:13.500
in the Saudi Arabia line
between Medina and Mecca.

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:15.480
From now on, I am
going to talk to you

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:17.400
about the new architecture
that are going

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.980
to be designed for the new
lines that Talgo is going

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:22.910
to employ all over the world.

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:24.800
So this architecture
has been designed,

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:27.540
and has been all right with
them over the last months.

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:31.390
And basically the
general view is this one.

00:25:31.390 --> 00:25:36.010
So we have designed this in mind
according to the requirements

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:37.920
that Talgo had.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:41.170
And Talgo had requirements
not only of data scaling,

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:42.638
of course, because
there are going

00:25:42.638 --> 00:25:44.680
to be more and more lines
with this architecture,

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:47.020
and these require quite an
escalation in terms of data.

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:49.350
But also, this has been
designed because there

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:53.000
are other devices where we need
to collect information from,

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:53.500
OK?

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:55.540
So, and these are
not only the trains.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:58.540
So for example, if
you see here, these

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:02.630
are the structures which are
equipped across the lines, OK?

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:05.390
So these are, like,
the wayside inspections

00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:07.940
that are collecting
information from the train

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:10.840
as the train is passing through
this particular structure, OK?

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:13.530
So, these are not
the train itself.

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:15.140
These are devices, OK?

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:19.670
So you can see here devices
that are put in the line

00:26:19.670 --> 00:26:23.470
and that they are getting
information from the train.

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:26.320
Also in the train itself, we
are interested in collecting not

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:27.910
only data information itself.

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:31.120
We are also interested in
collecting multimedia files,

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:35.050
for example, images
and also audios.

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:36.590
Because audios are
very interesting

00:26:36.590 --> 00:26:39.090
in order to detect
problems in the train, OK?

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:41.610
So if you listen to,
for example, the engine

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:45.780
of the train, you can make
predictive maintenance

00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:48.650
according to a particular audio.

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.900
So multimedia files are
also very, very interesting.

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:53.590
And the new requirements
include the collection

00:26:53.590 --> 00:26:55.760
of this type of information.

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:58.150
So, this new
architecture basically

00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:01.010
includes three new elements.

00:27:01.010 --> 00:27:03.130
The first element
is Cloud IoT Core.

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:07.060
So Cloud IoT Core now
is going to be included.

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:09.470
And We're going to
use MQTT in order

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:11.350
to send the messages
to the train.

00:27:11.350 --> 00:27:14.370
And also, we are going
to use TLS 1.2 in order

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:17.950
to make the transportation.

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:19.795
We are going to add
also some processing

00:27:19.795 --> 00:27:23.390
at the Edge, so some
processing in the train itself.

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:27.090
So now, we are going to design
some customized software

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.220
in order to be equipped
inside the train,

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:34.060
and also hardware that we are
going to put inside the train

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:35.560
in order to make,
for example, well,

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:37.268
machine learning,
predictive maintenance,

00:27:37.268 --> 00:27:39.160
and some other stuff.

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:41.680
And finally, the
third piece we are

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:44.770
going to add to the architecture
is Cloud ML Engine in order

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:48.340
to make all the training that
is required with the data

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:50.020
that we are collecting,
and also to be

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:55.790
able to download all the models
that are training in the cloud

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:58.282
to the devices in
the train itself.

00:27:58.282 --> 00:27:59.740
So these are the
three key elements

00:27:59.740 --> 00:28:01.930
that are going to be added.

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:08.080
Let me go a little bit
deeper into these two--

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:11.928
into the Cloud IoT Core on the
cloud and the Edge processing.

00:28:11.928 --> 00:28:13.720
The Cloud Machine
Learning Engine, I think,

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:16.180
is very well-known
product from Google Cloud.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:17.960
So I will skip that
in this presentation.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:20.770
So let me focus on these two.

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:22.745
So, Cloud IoT Core--

00:28:22.745 --> 00:28:24.495
I mean, it's a product
that is already GA,

00:28:24.495 --> 00:28:26.300
and basically it provides
two capabilities.

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:30.100
It's a message broker for
HTTP and MQTT messages.

00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:35.130
It provides also security by
implementing TLS 1.2 mandatory

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:38.140
in all the communications,
and also provides

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:39.510
device management capability.

00:28:39.510 --> 00:28:43.120
So somehow, it's an inventory
of all the essentials

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:47.960
and subsystems that are
tracked in the train.

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:51.230
One of the characteristics
of Cloud IoT Core

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:53.540
is that it's
completely serverless

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:56.390
and has a unique
global access point.

00:28:56.390 --> 00:28:59.630
So no matter if Talgo is
operating in Saudi Arabia,

00:28:59.630 --> 00:29:02.930
in the US, or in
the United Kingdom--

00:29:02.930 --> 00:29:05.990
all the information is going
to be sent to the Google Cloud

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:08.652
through the closest data center.

00:29:08.652 --> 00:29:10.360
And this is going to
happen automatically

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:13.280
without Talgo intervention.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:16.840
So this is about
the Cloud IoT Core.

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:18.660
And one of the most
interesting part

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:21.800
is the processing we have
designed for the Edge.

00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:26.090
In this slide, that you can
see two parts, one in the left

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:27.210
and one in the right.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:30.050
The right is just basically
a summary of the cloud

00:29:30.050 --> 00:29:31.350
piece for machine learning.

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:34.010
So I am going to skip
this part because it's

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:35.480
more or less
obvious and does not

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:38.060
contain all the details
of the previous slides.

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.970
I'm going to focus more
on this particular part--

00:29:40.970 --> 00:29:42.800
on the left part,
which is the part

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:45.840
which is going to be
equipped in the train.

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:50.010
So in the train, we
have an Ethernet bus,

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:51.170
which is here, OK?

00:29:51.170 --> 00:29:53.350
So this Ethernet
bus is basically

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:56.330
the bus which is collecting all
type of signals in the train.

00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:57.800
So it's a very--

00:29:57.800 --> 00:30:02.600
I mean, it's a very
important part of the train

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:05.180
because it's collecting
all kind of signals.

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:06.800
Some of these signals
are going to be

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:09.500
sent to the software we
have designed in the Edge,

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:12.990
and are going to be sent through
a path protected by a diode,

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:13.490
OK?

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:16.880
So this diode is going
to prevent the signals

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:19.860
from going from the
software to the bus, OK?

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:24.320
So it will only allow
signals from the Ethernet bus

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:27.050
to our software part.

00:30:27.050 --> 00:30:31.820
Then this piece of software is
going to be designed for Talgo.

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:34.350
And it's basically
a software based

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:37.060
on a microservices architecture.

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:39.990
So we are going to design
a container-based solution

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:41.560
for this particular part.

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:43.990
And basically what this
software piece has,

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:47.130
first of all, a interface
software, which is-- this

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:47.940
is not a container.

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:50.790
This is basically,
well, firmware

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:53.310
which is collecting
the information coming

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:54.660
from the Ethernet bus.

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:57.720
And then this is going
to send information

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:01.530
to the container that
will receive the signals.

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:05.130
Then this container
must exist mandatory.

00:31:05.130 --> 00:31:07.950
The other container that
must exist also mandatory

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:09.840
is what we call
"Container ZERO," which

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.810
is the one which is sending
the information to the cloud--

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:15.210
sending and receiving
information to the cloud.

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:19.040
So this will send all kind of
events, alarms, variables, et

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:21.990
cetera, as well as it will
download any machine learning

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:25.460
models coming from the cloud.

00:31:25.460 --> 00:31:28.490
And then optionally,
we should have

00:31:28.490 --> 00:31:31.410
one or more extra containers
for different tasks.

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:33.260
For example, we can
have a container

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:35.570
in order to select
the variables that we

00:31:35.570 --> 00:31:37.550
are going to send to the cloud.

00:31:37.550 --> 00:31:40.190
Or for example, this
container can also

00:31:40.190 --> 00:31:44.300
make some machine learning
activities if required.

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:46.820
The design-based
container is basically

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:48.930
a little bit open on
this particular part

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:53.510
because we can design a software
with three or four containers.

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:55.890
But basically the architecture
is based on containers

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:58.610
in order to facilitate
isolation and in order

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:00.570
to facilitate scalability.

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:02.230
So for a high speed
train like this,

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:03.470
security is very important.

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:06.200
I mean, you can imagine that
these trains cannot fail, OK?

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:09.215
So these trains-- everything
must work perfect.

00:32:09.215 --> 00:32:11.390
And a containerized
mesh application

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:13.230
fits in this
particular requirement.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:16.280
So that's the reason why we
have this service architecture

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:18.980
for this particular part.

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:20.960
So this container
solution is going

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:25.160
to be built on a Linux
OS operating system.

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:28.353
And then below that, we
will have the hardware part.

00:32:28.353 --> 00:32:30.020
And the hardware part
initially is going

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:32.980
to be designed with a CPU--

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:36.020
I mean, basically with a
CPU, and optionally, we

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:39.920
are going to place-- we are
going to host a space for TPUs.

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:41.870
Because we think
that the use cases

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:45.720
for this particular big
issue may require the TPUs,

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:50.330
especially for the
multimedia processing.

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:52.570
So, that's basically
the design that

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:54.550
is going to implemented
in the train.

00:32:54.550 --> 00:32:56.530
So a microservice
architecture built

00:32:56.530 --> 00:32:59.410
on software and hardware
that needs to be, of course,

00:32:59.410 --> 00:33:02.910
moderated by Talgo.

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:05.550
For the hardware
part, the Edge TPU--

00:33:05.550 --> 00:33:07.500
I mean, just for
your information,

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:11.790
we have recently released
through our partner, Coral,

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:16.950
some TPUs already available
in system-on-modules

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:17.850
architectures, OK?

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:21.320
So you can see if you go
to the web page of Coral,

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:25.510
this partner of Google
has released some hardware

00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:29.610
cards with TPU inside with
different hardware schemes.

00:33:29.610 --> 00:33:31.440
So we can use a
system on module.

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:34.150
And also you can
use PCI accelerators

00:33:34.150 --> 00:33:35.920
to replace any card.

00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:38.340
So there are different
options to install

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:39.840
this kind of hardware.

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:46.930
So, with this design in mind,
how would the architectures

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:48.350
look like?

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:51.330
So the architecture for
the new speed trains

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:52.330
look like the following.

00:33:52.330 --> 00:33:55.150
For historical
analysis, the changes

00:33:55.150 --> 00:33:57.700
are that we are
going now to-- well,

00:33:57.700 --> 00:33:59.720
we are still using
Wi-Fi in order

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:03.100
to send the historical
databases to the cloud.

00:34:03.100 --> 00:34:06.460
You may notice that there is
no local computer anymore, OK?

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:08.650
Because we have installed
the cloud as a gate.

00:34:08.650 --> 00:34:09.715
And gsutil in the train.

00:34:09.715 --> 00:34:12.560
So the train will send data
readily to cloud storage.

00:34:12.560 --> 00:34:14.480
Also, the train should
be equipped with 5G.

00:34:14.480 --> 00:34:16.230
And the reason for
that is because imagine

00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:17.730
that the train, for
whatever reason,

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:20.420
is stopped in the
middle of the train.

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:23.080
So we should be able to send
information very quickly

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:24.380
to a facility for processing.

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:28.708
So 5G should operate
also as well with Wi-Fi.

00:34:28.708 --> 00:34:30.750
And the amount of information
we're going to send

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.460
are not only the
SQLite databases,

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:35.620
but also the
multimedia files are

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:38.350
going to be sent to
the cloud storage.

00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:40.420
So the rest will be
more or less the same.

00:34:40.420 --> 00:34:41.850
Cloud Function--
a Cloud Function

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:45.442
here will trigger a function
that will start a Dataflow

00:34:45.442 --> 00:34:47.650
pipeline in order to collect
all the information that

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:50.442
will be sent to BigQuery
for visualization,

00:34:50.442 --> 00:34:52.150
and potentially to
Cloud Machine Learning

00:34:52.150 --> 00:34:53.520
to train a new model we require.

00:34:56.239 --> 00:34:59.850
And the second-- so to
summarize, so Wi-Fi or 5G data

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:01.480
is going to be sent
to cloud storage

00:35:01.480 --> 00:35:04.600
and then to Cloud Functions
for extracting the information

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:07.690
to cloud storage,
and then processing

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:10.040
and the Dataflow and
machine learning,

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:13.880
and finally to visualize.

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:18.740
And the architecture related to
real-time processing and event

00:35:18.740 --> 00:35:21.570
data management are going merge.

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:24.980
So the two previous one are
going to merge into just one.

00:35:24.980 --> 00:35:26.750
And in this particular
case, we are

00:35:26.750 --> 00:35:32.540
going to use MQTT and also the
batch information for events

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:33.353
and alarms.

00:35:33.353 --> 00:35:35.520
And this time, we are going
to use Dataflow in order

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:38.270
to unify the processing
of real time and batch,

00:35:38.270 --> 00:35:41.380
the operation where we
unify both architectures.

00:35:41.380 --> 00:35:45.180
So, all the data will go
to Cloud IoT Core, then

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:46.290
a Cloud Function.

00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:47.960
So, we'll create a
function in order

00:35:47.960 --> 00:35:54.302
to be processed by Pub/Sub and
go to Dataflow and AppEngine.

00:35:54.302 --> 00:35:55.760
I would like to
highlight something

00:35:55.760 --> 00:35:59.530
about this architecture, which
is the serverless approach,

00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:01.490
and especially the
usage of Dataflow

00:36:01.490 --> 00:36:05.990
here for processing
all the information.

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:07.430
This is very
interesting for Talgo

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:12.530
because Dataflow allows
scaling up and scaling down.

00:36:12.530 --> 00:36:16.330
So during the day,
trains are operating.

00:36:16.330 --> 00:36:18.890
And you need to process
a lot of information.

00:36:18.890 --> 00:36:22.400
Dataflow can scale up in order
to process more information

00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:23.750
if required.

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:26.960
But during the night when
trains do not operate,

00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:29.330
basically Dataflow
can scale down

00:36:29.330 --> 00:36:33.050
and Talgo is going to pay
nothing for processing, OK?

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:36.320
Because Dataflow
allows scaling up

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:39.090
and scaling down automatically.

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:42.000
So that means that
during the day

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:45.130
while the trains are operating
between the different lines,

00:36:45.130 --> 00:36:46.630
trains are going
to send information

00:36:46.630 --> 00:36:48.670
and Dataflow is
going to scale up.

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:51.060
But during the night when
trains are not operating,

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:53.900
Dataflow is going to
automatically scale down

00:36:53.900 --> 00:36:56.760
and Talgo will not pay
anything for the Dataflow

00:36:56.760 --> 00:37:00.610
or for Cloud IoT Core
or for Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:37:00.610 --> 00:37:04.800
So this is the advantage
of a serverless approach.

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:07.710
This is an advantage of
a serverless architecture

00:37:07.710 --> 00:37:08.880
like this, OK?

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:12.240
So all this solution is going
to scale up and scale down

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:13.330
automatically.

00:37:13.330 --> 00:37:16.890
And I make a remark on the
scaling down automatically.

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:19.410
I mean, Talgo is not going to
do anything during the night

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:22.680
in order to switch
off the machines

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:25.030
or switch off the workers
in order to pay less.

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:29.590
So this is going to be done
automatically by Google Cloud.

00:37:29.590 --> 00:37:33.140
Another advantage like
this is that the Dataflow

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:38.390
is going to unify the processing
model of batch and streaming.

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:40.850
So actually, the
same probe is going

00:37:40.850 --> 00:37:43.380
to make both type
of processing, OK?

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:45.980
Batch for events and
alarms, and processing

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:48.620
for real-time information.

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:50.630
So to summarize
this architecture,

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:53.530
so we are going to send
information in batches

00:37:53.530 --> 00:37:55.790
and then streaming
into the cloud.

00:37:55.790 --> 00:37:58.670
That will go to-- some
of them to the IoT Core.

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:01.750
And then Dataflow will provide
the serverless approach

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:04.680
with an automatically
scaling up and scaling down

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:08.230
whenever it is required, and
the data stored on AppEngine

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:11.560
for visualization

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:14.110
So in this architecture,
we have machine learning.

00:38:14.110 --> 00:38:17.080
And actually Talgo
is working today

00:38:17.080 --> 00:38:19.480
on some machine learning models
that Jose Antonio's going

00:38:19.480 --> 00:38:24.430
to explain in a second that are
actually being worked today.

00:38:24.430 --> 00:38:26.835
So, now I'm going to pass
the word to Jose Antonio

00:38:26.835 --> 00:38:28.960
because he's going to
explain us a machine learning

00:38:28.960 --> 00:38:31.930
predictive maintenance case
which we are using today

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:33.692
in the Saudi Arabia line.

00:38:33.692 --> 00:38:35.650
JOSE ANTONIO MARCOS
ALBERCA: Thank you, Rafael.

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:46.590
OK, now I'm going to talk about
the Talgo Tesla application.

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.620
This application
has been developed

00:38:48.620 --> 00:38:50.960
using machine
learning to predict

00:38:50.960 --> 00:38:56.240
the failures of different
system of the train in advance.

00:38:56.240 --> 00:39:00.050
As an example, I'm
going to explain you

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:03.440
how the application
is capable to predict

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:06.035
the failures of
the axle bearings

00:39:06.035 --> 00:39:08.630
in advance at least three weeks.

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:16.090
OK, it works in this way.

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:18.750
The axle of the
train you can see

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:24.410
in the top part of the
screen has four bearings.

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:28.180
Each bearing has a
temperature sensor.

00:39:28.180 --> 00:39:34.040
Every temperature sensor is
being monitored in real time.

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:39.280
So the neural network, using
Keras library and TensorFlow

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:44.980
backend, predict the
temperature for its sensor

00:39:44.980 --> 00:39:50.575
every second using the other
three real-time temperatures,

00:39:50.575 --> 00:39:55.190
or the other three sensors
plus the outside temperature.

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:58.400
Once the temperature
is predicted,

00:39:58.400 --> 00:40:02.690
the application is
comparing this temperature

00:40:02.690 --> 00:40:04.590
with the real one.

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:09.880
If the difference between
them is higher than expected,

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:13.510
the application
provides an alarm.

00:40:13.510 --> 00:40:16.920
And also the most
important thing

00:40:16.920 --> 00:40:20.670
is that we are able to
predict the failure in advance

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:23.790
at least three weeks
in order to avoid

00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:26.760
to have some failure in the
train during the service

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:32.910
and to do the maintenance
as preventive maintenance.

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:37.330
OK, in this screen, you
can see in the upper part

00:40:37.330 --> 00:40:41.950
the general overview of
the train with the axles.

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:46.750
For example, this axle,
this bearing, has an alarm.

00:40:46.750 --> 00:40:51.340
That means that this difference
between the real temperature,

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:53.890
which is the blue,
with the calculated one

00:40:53.890 --> 00:40:55.570
is higher than the limit.

00:40:55.570 --> 00:41:01.240
So the application gets an alarm
to be fixed for the maintenance

00:41:01.240 --> 00:41:03.074
people and for the engineers.

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:11.770
And now we are going to see a
video with all the information

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:13.029
that I present before.

00:41:13.029 --> 00:41:13.696
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:41:13.696 --> 00:41:17.098
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:26.820
So, we are performing
real-time monitoring

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:31.170
in very extreme conditions,
weather conditions, Kazakhstan,

00:41:31.170 --> 00:41:34.265
or in the middle of the
desert, Saudi Arabia.

00:41:37.210 --> 00:41:39.780
We are sending the
data from the center

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:42.580
of the train to the cloud.

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:48.680
We are streaming 2,000
signals per second

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:53.220
from different trains, different
countries, to the cloud.

00:41:56.960 --> 00:41:59.000
So once we have
the data there, we

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:05.070
are able to analyze the
data with our expertise.

00:42:05.070 --> 00:42:07.720
And we can get
insight from the data

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:11.620
in order to improve the
safety, the reliability

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:15.650
and the availability
of the train.

00:42:15.650 --> 00:42:21.080
Unlike in F1, we have to perform
the maintenance as needed,

00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:25.730
using machine learning
and real-time monitoring

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:29.331
to improve the safety,
reliability, and maintenance.

00:42:33.660 --> 00:42:38.960
[END PLAYBACK]

00:42:38.960 --> 00:42:40.590
And now, what's next?

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:46.970
To connect not only
the train, but also

00:42:46.970 --> 00:42:52.390
all the assets that
Talgo has in a global IoT

00:42:52.390 --> 00:42:57.615
platform using our application
Talgo Tesla and Google

00:42:57.615 --> 00:42:58.240
Cloud solution.

00:43:01.640 --> 00:43:06.380
For example, we are developing
a new equipment called TALVI,

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:10.010
capable to inspect the
train automatically

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:13.390
when the train is running
up to 100 miles per hour

00:43:13.390 --> 00:43:16.280
on the track.

00:43:16.280 --> 00:43:21.010
This equipment has
3D scan technology

00:43:21.010 --> 00:43:27.350
and visual artificial cameras
to inspect the wheel parameters,

00:43:27.350 --> 00:43:32.480
the side integrity of the train,
the brakes, the frame, and also

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:34.190
the roof inspections.

00:43:34.190 --> 00:43:40.785
So all this we are able to
do thanks to the IoT Cloud

00:43:40.785 --> 00:43:41.285
platform.

00:43:43.940 --> 00:43:45.980
So that's all.

00:43:45.980 --> 00:43:47.120
Thank you.

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:50.830
And I hope that it has
been interesting for you

00:43:50.830 --> 00:43:53.820
and useful maybe for
your business case.

00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:54.320
Thank you.

